  "subtitle": "A forest machine whose computerized harvester head measures each tree in the dark while it is being eaten — caliper-as-interface, deciding where to cut before the operator ever sees the log.",
  "why_scout_cares": "The interaction is a genuine closed measurement loop with a living organism: the harvester head seizes a standing tree, fells it, then feeds the stem through its delimbing knives while a measuring wheel counts every centimeter — and, from 1986, a diameter sensor reads the stem's thickness. A control computer in the cab uses those two signals to compute volume and to decide the bucking points (where to cut the stem into saleable logs) before a single log is dropped. The operator is no longer measuring with eyes and a tape; the machine is a caliper you sit inside, and the tree is consumed as data. This is the museum's first agricultural/forestry production machine, its first 'measuring-while-destroying' instrument, and a distinct topology from the crop-yield and lab-measurement exhibits: the actuator (the shears) and the sensor (the measuring wheel) are the same hand holding the same piece of matter.",
